## Synchronous defect passivation of all-inorganic perovskite solar cells enabled by fullerene interlayer
Yanbo Shang<sup>1</sup>, Pu Wang<sup>1</sup>, Lingbo Jia<sup>1</sup>, Xingcheng Li<sup>1</sup>, Weitao Lian<sup>1</sup>, Peisen Qian<sup>1</sup>, Muqing Chen<sup>2</sup>, Tao Chen<sup>1</sup>, Yalin Lu<sup>1</sup>, and Shangfeng Yang<sup>1</sup> (✉)
<sup>1</sup> CAS Key Laboratory of Materials for Energy Conversion, Anhui Laboratory of Advanced Photon Science and Technology, Department of Materials Science and Engineering, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei 230026, China  <sup>2</sup> School of Environment and Civil Engineering, Dongguan University of Technology, Dongguan 523808, China
## ABSTRACT
All-inorganic \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3-x}\mathsf{B r}_{x}\) perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are advantageous in terms of high thermal stability, while its efficiency lags behind those of organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite counterparts. Defect passivations have been extensively applied for enhancing efficiency of all-inorganic PSCs, which are mainly based on univocal defect passivation of perovskite layer. Herein, we incorporated a bis-dimethylamino-functionalized fullerene derivative (abbreviated as PCBDMAM) as an interlayer between ZnO electron transport layer (ETL) and all-inorganic \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{2-x}\mathsf{B r}_{0.75}\) perovskite layer, accomplishing synchronous defect passivations of both layers and consequently dramatic enhancements of efficiency and thermal stability of PSC devices. Upon spin-coating PCBDMAM onto ZnO ETL, the surface defects of ZnO especially oxygen vacancies can be effectively passivated due to the formation of \(Z\mathsf{n}{-}\mathsf{N}\) ionic bonds. In addition, PCBDMAM incorporation affords effective passivation of \(\mathsf{P b}_{1}\) and \(\mathsf{I}_{\mathsf{P b}}\) antisite defects within the atop perovskite layer as well via coordination bonding with \(\mathsf{P b}^{2+}\). As a result, the regular-structure planar \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{2.25}\mathsf{B r}_{0.75}\) PSC device delivers a champion power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \(17.04\%\), which surpasses that of the control device \((15.44\%)\). Moreover, the PCBDMAM-incorporated PSC device maintains \(\sim80\%\) of its initial PCE after \(600~\mathrm{h}\) heating at \(85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) hot plate in \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) atmosphere, whereas PCE of the control device degrades rapidly to \(\sim62\%\) after \(460~\mathrm{h}\) heating under identical conditions. Hence, PCBDMAM incorporation benefited dramatic improvement of the thermal stability of PSC device.
## KEYWORDS
all-inorganic perovskite solar cells, fullerene derivative, electron transport layer, thermal stability, defect passivation
## 1 Introduction
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have been attracting worldwide interests with the certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) rising to \(25.7\%\) [1]. High efficiency PSC devices are mostly based on organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite light-absorbing materials, which are however intrinsically volatile and thermally unstable due to the existence of organic cations, resulting in poor thermal stability of PSC devices and hampering large-scale commercialization of organic-inorganic hybrid PSCs [2, 3]. Hence, developing all-inorganic \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3-x}\mathsf{B r}_{x}\) \((x=0{-}5)\) PSCs with higher thermal stability than organic-inorganic hybrid counterparts is needed [4, 5]. Among the reported all-inorganic \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3-x}\mathsf{B r}_{x}\) PSCs, iodine-rich \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3-x}\mathsf{B r}_{x}\) \((0<x\leq1)\) perovskites bear a reasonable balance between bandgap and phase stability because partial substitution of \(\Gamma\) anion with smaller \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) is the most effective way to stabilize the perovskite phase [6–8]. The highest PCE of \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3-x}\mathsf{B r}_{x}\) \((0<x\leq1)\) PSCs without any organic salt additive reaches \(18.43\%\) [7], which obviously lags behind those of organic-inorganic hybrid PSCs. To enhance PCE of \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3-x}\mathsf{B r}_{x}\) \((0<x\leq1)\) PSCs, interface engineering has been extensively applied and demonstrated to be effective in promoting interfacial charge transport via improving film morphology, lowering the energy level mismatch and passivating the traps in perovskite. In particular, by using different types of enthalters including small molecules [9, 10], polymers [11, 12], inorganic compounds [13, 14], 2D perovskite layers [15, 16] as well as fullerene and its derivatives [17–20], defect passivations of all-inorganic \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3-x}\mathsf{B r}_{x}\) \((0<x\leq1)\) PSCs have been accomplished. Among the reported interlayer passivators, few fullerene derivatives such as [6,6]-phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) featuring strong electron-accepting abilities have been implemented to modify ZnO [20] and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) [17, 18] electron transport layer (ETL), leading to not only passivated trap states of perovskite surface but also enhanced electron transport. For instance, Duan et al. pre-treated \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL with fullerene-ethylenediamine
additive reaches \(18.43\%\) [7], which obviously lags behind those of organic-inorganic hybrid PSCs. To enhance PCE of \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3-x}\mathsf{B r}_{x}\) \((0\,<\,x\,\leq\,1)\) ) PSCs, interface engineering has been extensively applied and demonstrated to be effective in promoting interfacial charge transport via improving film morphology, lowering the energy level mismatch and passivating the traps in perovskite. In particular, by using different types of enthalters including small molecules [9, 10], polymers [11, 12], inorganic compounds [13, 14], 2D perovskite layers [15, 16] as well as fullerene and its derivatives [17−20], defect passivations of all-inorganic \(\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3-x}\mathsf{B r}_{x}\) \((0<x\leq1)\) ) PSCs have been accomplished. Among the reported interlayer passivators, few fullerene derivatives such as [6,6]-phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) featuring strong electron-accepting abilities have been implemented to modify \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) [20] and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) [17, 18] electron transport layer (ETL), leading to not only passivated trap states of perovskite surface but also enhanced electron transport. For instance, Duan et al. pre-treated \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL with fullerene-ethylenediamine
derivatives \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{60}\mathrm{EDA}\right.\) and \(\left.\mathrm{C}_{70}\mathrm{EDA}\right)\), inducing large-grain \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}\) film with low defect density and superior interface connectivity, as a result a decent PCE of \(16.58\%\) was obtained [18]. Yao et al. used PCBM to passivated trap states of n-i-p \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}\) PSCs, effectively suppressing hysteresis of devices [17]. Liu et al. tailored \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) ETL via tris(pentafluorophenyl)borane (TPFPB) and \(\mathrm{LiClO}_{4}\) codoping strategy, leading to enhanced electron injection and reduced charge recombination [19]. However, these fullerene passivators mainly result in univocal defect passivation of perovskite layer and consequently limited performance improvement.
Herein, a bis-dimethylamino-functionalized fullerene derivative (abbreviated as PCBDMAM) was incorporated as an interlayer between \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) electron transport layer (ETL) and all-inorganic \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite layer, resulting in synchronous defect passivations of both layers as confirmed by a series of morphological and spectral characterizations. After incorporating PCBDMAM interlayer, PCE of the regular-structure planar \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) PSC device is increased obviously from \(15.44\%\) to \(17.04\%\) along with dramatic improvement of the thermal stability.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Zinc acetate dehydrate \((99.5\%)\), ethanolamine, and 2-methoxyethanol \((99.8\%)\) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, \(>\ 99.8\%\)), dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO, \(>\ 99.8\%\)), chlorobenzene \((99.9\%)\), ethanolamine \((>99.9\%)\), 2-methoxyethanol \((>99.0\%)\) and lead bromide \((\mathrm{PbBr}_{2}, 99.999\%)\) were purchased from Alfa Aesar. Cesium iodide (CsI, \(99.99\%\)) and tris(2-(1H-pyrazol-1-yl)-4-tert-butylpyridine) cobalt(iii)-tris(bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide) (FK209) were purchased from Xi'an Polymer Light Technology Corp. (PLT). Lead iodide \((\mathrm{PbI}_{2}, 99.99\%)\) were purchased from TCI. Ethanol, isopropanol, acetone, and acetonitrile were all purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. 2,2',7,7'-Tetrakis[N,N-di(4-methoxyphenyl)amino]-9,9'-spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD) was purchased from 1 M company Ltd. Lithium bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl) imide (Li-TFSI \(99\%\)) was purchased from Aladdin. 4-Tertbutylpyridine (t-BP \(97\%\)) was purchased from Adamas Reagent. Synthesis process of PCBDMAM was reported in our previous work [21].
## 2.2 Device fabrication
The FTO-coated glass substrate with a sheet resistance of \(10\;\Omega{\cdot}\mathbf{s}\mathbf{q}^{-1}\) was ultrasonicated in detergent, deionized water, acetone, and isopropanol for \(15\,\mathrm{\min}\) before being dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . The \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) precursor solution was prepared according to literature [22]. The fresh films were put into \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) glove box to annealed at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) and then at \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in air. For PCBDMAM modification, the PCBDMAM solution in chlorobenzene was spun coated on \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) substrate at \(4000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\;{\mathrm{s}}\) and then annealed at \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) . The \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) precursor solution (comprised of \(1.1\mathrm{~M~}\) CsI, 0.6875M \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2},\) \(0.4125\mathrm{~M~PbBr}_{2}\) in \(700~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) DMF and \(300~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) DMSO) was spin coated on the substrate at \(2900\ \mathrm{rpm}\) for \(40~\mathrm{s}\) . Then the prepared precursor films were thermally annealed by a two-step process at \(55\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(60\,s\) and \(255\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) .
For the deposition of hole transporting layer, Spiro-OMeTAD \((73.2\;\mathrm{mg})\) was first dissolved in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of chlorobenzene, and then \(29\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of t-BP and \(18.8~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of Li-TFSI solution \((520\;\mathrm{mg}\) Li-TFSI in
For the deposition of hole transporting layer, Spiro-OMeTAD \((73.2\;\mathrm{mg})\) was first dissolved in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of chlorobenzene, and then \(29\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of t-BP and \(18.8~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of Li-TFSI solution \((520\;\mathrm{mg}\) Li-TFSI in \(1\;\mathrm{mL}\) of acetonitrile) as well as \(29\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of a tris(2-(1H-pyrazol-1-yl)-4-tert-butylpyridine)cobalt(iii)-tris(bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl) imide) (FK209) stock solution \((300\;\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) in acetonitrile) were added. The Spiro-OMeTAD solution was spin-coated onto the perovskite layer at \(3000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\;{\mathrm{s}}.\) Finally, \(65\;\mathrm{nm}\) gold (Au) was thermally evaporated under vacuum \((\approx10^{-6}\) Torr) respectively. A shadow mask was employed to determine the effective device area of \(0.09\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) . All device fabrication procedures were carried out in a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -purged glovebox \((<0.1\;\mathrm{ppm}\) of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{LiO}\) ).
## 2.3 Measurements and characterization
The current density voltage \((J{-}V)\) characterizations were measured by a Keithley 2400 source measurement unit under simulated AM 1.5 irradiation \((100\;\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-2}})\) with a standard xenon-lamp-based solar simulator (XEC-300M2), which was calibrated with a monocrystalline silicon reference cell (Oriel P/N 91 150 V, with KG-5 visible color filter) calibrated by the National Renewable Energy Laboratory. The measurements were performed with a mask (well-defined area of size \(0.09\,\mathrm{\cm}^{2})\) to ensure accurate measurement under ambient conditions. Around 20 devices were fabricated and measured independently under each experimental condition to warrant the reproducibility of the result and to obtain the statistical data of the photovoltaic parameters. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) measurements were performed on an ORIEL Intelligent Quantum Efficiency 200TM Measurement system established with a tunable light source.
## 3 Results and discussion
3 Results and discussionBis-dimethylamino-functionalized fullerene derivative PCBDMAM was first synthesized by our group via a facile esterification reaction of [6,6]-phenyl-C61-butyric acid (PCBA) with bis(4-(2-(dimethylamino) ethoxy) phenyl) methanol (DMAM-OH), and incorporated as an auxiliary fullerene interlayer atop of PCBM to form a PCBM/PCBDMAM double fullerene cathode buffer layer (CBL) of inverted (p-i-n) planar \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) PSCs as reported previously [21]. The nitrogen atom in PCBDMAM can bond with Ag cathode so that an interfacial dipole layer formed between PCBM and Ag cathode, resulting in decrease of the work function of the Ag cathode [21]. Stimulated by this work, we managed to incorporate PCBDMAM between ZnO ETL and \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite layer in the present work. The decomposition temperature of PCBDMAM is determined to be beyond \(392.3\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) [21], such a high thermal stability ensures its applicability in ZnO-based \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) PSC devices.
PCBDMAM was dissolved in chlorobenzene with variable concentrations (1, 2, and \(5~\mathrm{{\mg\cdotmL^{-1}}}\) ) and then spin-coated onto thermally annealed ZnO layer. We first employed AFM to investigate the influence of PCBDMAM modification on the morphology ZnO film. Figures 1(a)–1(d) compares the AFM images of the PCBDMAM-modified and bare \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) films. The bare \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) film exhibits a rough surface composed of stripes, and the root-mean-square (RMS) roughness reaches \(9.26\,\mathrm{\nm}\) . Upon modification with low-concentration PCBDMAM (1 and \(2\,\mathrm{\mg\cdotmL^{-1}}\) ), the surface looks smoother with the RMS roughness decreases to 6.25 and \(2.61\ \mathrm{nm}\) , and the valleys between the stripes become smaller and finally disappear. The smoother surface is beneficial for improving the interfacial contact between the ZnO ETL and perovskite layer [23]. However, when the concentration of PCBDMAM increases to \(5~\mathrm{mg\cdotmL^{-1}}\) , the RMS roughness increases to \(4.13~\mathrm{\nm}\) due likely to aggregation of PCBDMAM molecules. In addition to the influence on the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) film morphology, PCBDMAM modification is also found to affect thewettabilityofZnOfilm.UponPCBDMAMmodification,theDMF contact angle of the \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) film increases dramatically from \(2.4^{\circ}\) to \(12.8^{\circ}\) \(\mathrm{~\leftmoon~}1\;\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}m L^{-1}}\right)\) , \(13.8^{\circ}\) \(\mathrm{2\,\mg{\cdot}m L^{-1}}\) ) and \(35.2^{\circ}\) \(\mathrm{~\leftmoon~}5\;\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}m L^{-1}}\right)\) , indicating that PCBDMAM modification improves the hydrophobicity of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) film because PCBDMAM is hydrophobic
To confirm the presence of PCBDMAM on the surface of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) and the influence of PCBDMAM on the defects of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) films, we carried out XPS characterizations. As seen from the N 1s spectra, the nitrogen signal peak at \(399.8\;\;\mathrm{eV}\) observed in PCBDMAM appears obviously in ZnO/PCBDMAM film (Fig. 1(e)), indicating the presence of PCBDMAM on \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) film. This peak shifts negatively to \(399.2~\mathrm{eV},\) suggesting electron transfer from \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) to PCBDMAM [24]. Besides, after \(2\ \ \mathrm{mg\cdotmL^{-1}}\) PCBDMAM modification, both \(Z\mathrm{n}\,2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) and \(Z\mathrm{n}\;2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) signals detected at 1044.3 and \(1021.2~\mathrm{eV}\) shift positively by \(0.3~\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. 1(f)). This suggests electron donation of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) , agreeing with the N 1s result. These results suggest electron transfer from \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) to PCBDMAM, very likely via the formation of \(Z\mathfrak{n}{-}\Nu\) ionic bonds. As a result, the surface defects of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) such as oxygen vacancies are expected to be passivated [24–26], as discussed further below.
To gain deeper insight into the specific defects of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) which are passivated by PCBDMAM modification, we quantitatively measured the deep traps of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) by using the DLTS. We fabricated Schottky-knot device with a structure of \(\mathrm{FTO/Au/ZnO/PCBDMAM/Au}\) [27]. Figure \(1(\mathrm{g})\) compares the DLTS spectra of the device with and without PCBDMAM modification, from which the activation energy \((E_{\mathrm{C}}\,-\,E_{\mathrm{T}})\) and concentration of trap \((N_{\mathrm{T}})\) can be obtained from Arrhenius plots and the fitting results (see Fig. S2 and Table S1 in the ESM). For the control device based on the pristine \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) , three positive peaks labelled as \(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Z1}}\) , \(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Z}2}\) and \(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Z}3}\) are observed, which are assigned to electron traps in n-type \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) films. According to the activation energies of the deep traps, \(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Z1}}\) , \(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Z}2}\) and \(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Z}3}\) are attributed respectively to the antisite defects \(Z\mathrm{n}_{\mathrm{S}}\) interstitial zinc \(Z\mathfrak{n}_{\mathrm{i}}\) and oxygen vacancies \(\mathrm{V_{O}}\) [28], among which \(\mathrm{V_{O}}\) as the most intense peak is the main point defect because of the largest \(\mathrm{N_{T}}\) value (see Table S1 in the ESM). Upon PCBDMAM modification, the latter two peaks become undetectable, and only \(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Z1}}\) \((Z\mathrm{n}_{\mathrm{O}})\) is observed with much lower \(\mathrm{N_{T}}\) value. This indicates that PCBDMAM
modification can effectively passivate \( \mathrm{V_{O}} \) and \( \mathrm{Zn_{i}} \) defects on \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) surface.
As an interlayer between ETL and perovskite in n-i-p PSCs, it is necessary to examine the influence of PCBDMAM incorporation on the optical transmittance of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) film. Optical transmission spectra of the bare and PCBDMAM modified \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) films are compared in Fig. S3 in the ESM, indicating that low-concentration (1 and \( 2\,\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}} \)) PCBDMAM incorporation leads to slight decrease of the optical transmittance of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) film in the short-wavelength range of 350−500 and \( 850{-}1100\ \mathrm{nm} \) due to the optical absorption of PCBDMAM, whereas obvious optical transmittance drop is observed when the PCBDMAM concentration increases to \( 5\,\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}} \). This phenomenon is similar to the cases of other fullerene passivators reported previously [29]. Fortunately power density of sunlight is not very high in this range, so slight optical transmittance loss induced by low-concentration PCBDMAM incorporation is expected to bring negligible influence on PSC device efficiency as discussed below.
Since perovskite film is deposited on top of \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) layer, it is necessary to investigate the influence of PCBDMAM incorporation on the morphology of the \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) perovskite film atop, which is characterized by SEM. The \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) perovskite film deposited on bare \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) substrate looks compact without discernible pinholes (Fig. 2(a)), and the average crystalline grain size is about \( 387.1\ \mathrm{nm} \) (see Fig. S4 in the ESM for grain size distribution) with vague grain boundaries. After low-concentration \( (1,\ 2\ \mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}) \) PCBDMAM incorporation, the grain boundaries appear clearer (Figs. 2(b) and 2(c)), and the average perovskite grain size increases to \( 458.3\ \mathrm{nm} \) \( (1\ \mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}) \) and \( 549.7\ \mathrm{nm} \) \( (2\ \mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}) \). When PCBDMAM concentration increases to \( 5\ \mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}} \), although the average perovskite grain size keeps on increasing \( (650.2\ \mathrm{nm}) \), obvious pinholes are observed (Fig. 2(d)). This is resulted from the increased hydrophobicity of \( \mathrm{ZnO/PCBDMAM} \) substrate as discussed above. On the other hand, according to a comparison of the cross-sectional SEM images of the perovskite films deposited on PCBDMAM-modified and bare \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) substrates, the largest crystal grains with perfectly vertical orientations are obtained when PCBDMAM concentration is \( 2\ \mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}} \) (see Fig. S5 in the ESM).
We next employed XRD to probe the influence of PCBDMAM incorporation on the crystallinity of the \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) perovskite film atop. XRD patterns of all perovskite films exhibit two intense
diffraction peaks at \( 14.46^{\circ} \) and \( 29.36^{\circ} \) assigned respectively to (100) and (200) lattice planes of \( \alpha \)-phase \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) [24]. After low-concentration (1 and \( 2\ \mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}} \)) PCBDMAM incorporation, the intensities of the two diffraction peaks both increase, and the highest intensities are observed when PCBDMAM concentration is \( 2\ \mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}} \) (Fig. 2(e)), suggesting that PCBDMAM incorporation under this concentration improves the crystallinity of the \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) perovskite film.
Since PCBDMAM is incorporated as an interlayer between \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) and \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) perovskite, it is also necessary to probe whether PCBDMAM can interact with the atop \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) perovskite as well. We then carried out XPS characterization to probe the change of the binding energy of \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) within \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) after PCBDMAM incorporation. According to a comparison of the Pb 4f XPS spectra of the pristine \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) and \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}/\mathrm{PCBDMAM} \) films (Fig. 2(f)), both Pb \( 4\mathrm{f}_{5/2} \) and Pb \( 4\mathrm{f}_{7/2} \) signals at 143.3 and \( 138.5\ \mathrm{eV} \) detected for the pristine \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) shift negatively to 142.9 and \( 138.1\ \mathrm{eV} \) respectively after PCBDMAM incorporation. Meanwhile N 1s signal of PCBDMAM shifts positively by 0.3 eV in the \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}/\mathrm{PCBDMAM} \) bilayer film (see Figs. S6 in the ESM). This suggests the increase of electron cloud density around \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) cations resulted from the donation of the lone electron pair on the N atoms of PCBDMAM to the empty 6p orbital of \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) via coordination bonding. Such coordination interactions are expected to benefit passivating the defects within \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) [30−32].
To unveil the specific defects of \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) perovskite to be passivated by PCBDMAM, we employed DLTS to quantitatively probe the deep traps of \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75} \) based on a device with a structure of \( \mathrm{FTO/ZnO/CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}/\mathrm{Spiro-OMeTAD/Au} \) [33]. According to a comparison of the DLTS spectra of the devices with and without PCBDMAM (Fig. 2(g), see also Fig. S7 and Table S2 in the ESM for the fitting results), two positive peaks labelled as \( \mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{P1}} \), \( \mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{P2}} \) are observed in the control device without PCBDMAM, which are attributed respectively to the antisite defects \( \mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{I}} \) and \( \mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{Pb}} \) as the common defects in \( \mathrm{CsPbI}_{x}\mathrm{Br}_{3-x} \) perovskite [34]. Upon PCBDMAM modification, the \( \mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{P1}} \) \( (\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{I}}) \) peak become invisible and only \( \mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{P2}} \) \( (\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{I}}) \) peak is observed with much lower \( \mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{T}} \) value, suggesting decreased trap concentration. This indicates that PCBDMAM modification can effectively
passivate the antisite defects in \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\). As a result, synchronous defect passivations of both ZnO ETL and \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) layer are achieved by incorporating PCBDMAM interlayer. This highlights the advantage of PCBDMAM passivator.
In order to check the influence of PCBDMAM incorporation on the performance of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) PSC, we constructed regular-structure (n-i-p) PSC devices with a structure of FTO/ ZnO/PCBDMAM/CsPbI2.25Br0.75/Spiro-OMeTAD/Au (Fig. 3(a)), in which Spiro-OMeTAD was applied as hole transport layer (HTL). Figure 3(b) compares the \(J{-}V\) curves of the champion devices with and without PCBDMAM interlayer measured under one sun illumination, and the photovoltaic parameters including open-circuit voltage \((V_{\infty})\), short-circuit current \((J_{\mathrm{sc}})\), fill factor (FF), PCE, series resistance \((R_{\mathrm{s}})\) and shunt resistance \((R_{\mathrm{sh}})\) are summarized in Table 1 (see Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM for box statistic plots of device parameters and PCE histograms). The control device based on bare \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) ETL exhibits a best PCE of \(15.44\%\) calculated from a \(V_{\infty}\) of \(1.177\mathrm{~V~}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(16.88\mathrm{~mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), and an FF of \(77.71\%\). Upon PCBDMAM modification, the PCE increases obviously to \(16.24\%\) (1 mg·mL\(^{-1}\)) and \(17.04\%\) (2 mg·mL\(^{-1}\)), which however decreases to \(15.65\%\) when the concentration of PCBDMAM increases to \(2\mathrm{~mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\). Accordingly, the optimized concentration of PCBDMAM is determined to be \(2\mathrm{~mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), under which \(V_{\infty}\) (from 1.177 to \(1.248\mathrm{~V~}\), \(\sim6.0\%\) enhancement) and FF (from \(77.71\%\) to \(83.45\%\), \(\sim7.4\%\) enhancement) increase
simultaneously relative to control device whereas \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) slightly decreased. The minor change of \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) is verified by EQE measurements (Fig. 3(c)), indicating slightly decreased integrated \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) originated from the optical absorption of PCBDMAM in the range of \(350{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}\) as mentioned above. Besides, the stabilized photocurrent and steady PCE for the champion PCBDMAM \((2\mathrm{~mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) device loaded at the maximum power point \((V_{\mathrm{mpp}}\approx1.05\ \mathrm{V})\) over \(300\ \mathrm{s}\) reach \(15.87\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and \(16.18\%\), respectively, which are obviously higher than those of the control device (see Fig. S10 in the ESM). Moreover, the hysteresis index defined as [PCE(reverse)-PCE(forward)]/PCE(reverse)] decreases from \(7.0\%\) to \(4.8\%\) for the champion PCBDMAM \((2\mathrm{~mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) device (see Fig.S11 and Table S3 in the ESM). Hence, the hysteresis is effectively suppressed after PCBDMAM incorporation but still exists likely due to light-induced phase segregation [35, 36].
We next used space charge limited current (SCLC) method based on an electron-only device with a structure of FTO/ZnO/PCBDMAM/CsPbI2.25Br0.75 perovskite/PCBM/Ag to quantitatively determine the electron trap-state density \((n_{\mathrm{t}})\). According to the comparison of the \(J{-}V\) curves for the devices with and without PCBDMAM (Fig. 3(d)), the measured \(n_{\mathrm{t}}\) value decreases from \(2.35\,\times\,10^{16}\,\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) (control) to \(1.72\,\times\,10^{16}\,\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) \((1\mathrm{~mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\), \(1.47\,\times\,10^{16}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) \((2\mathrm{~mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) and \(1.98\,\times\,10^{16}\,\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) \((5\mathrm{~mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) after PCBDMAM modification (see Table S4 in the ESM for detailed analyses). This confirms that trap states within
\(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) layer can be effectively passivated via PCBDMAM incorporation. In order to uncover the factors accounting for the increases of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and FF, we carried out a series of characterizations to elucidate the influence of PCBDMAM incorporation on the charge carrier dynamics of the device. The electron extraction and electron–hole recombination dynamics at the \(Z\mathrm{nO}/\) perovskite interface is first investigated by steady-state and TRPL spectroscopies. Figure 3(e) compares the steady-state PL spectra of the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite films deposited on of the bare and PCBDMAM modified \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) substrates measured under an excitation wavelength of \(460~\mathrm{nm}\), revealing that the characteristic PL peak at \(667~\mathrm{nm}\) of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite deposited on the bare \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) is quenched after PCBDMAM modification, and the highest quenching ratio is observed for \(2\;\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) PCBDMAM. In addition, such a PL peak exhibits a blue-shift from 667 to \(664\;\mathrm{nm}\) \((2\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}m L^{-1}}\) PCBDMAM). These results solidify passivation of the trap states of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) [23]. Furthermore, as obtained from TRPL spectra shown in Fig. 3(f), the carrier lifetimes \((\tau)\) of the \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite films deposited on 1, 2, and \(5~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) PCBDMAM modified \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) are 17.69, 13.96, and 21.81 ns, respectively, which are all smaller than that on the bare \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) \((48.48~\mathrm{ns})\) (see Table S5 in the ESM). The decrease of \(\tau\) value indicates that PCBDMAM incorporation promotes effectively electron extraction from perovskite to \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) ETL, contributing to the increases of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and FF [37]. We then investigate the dependence of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) on light intensities \((I)\) so as to understand further the charge recombination kinetics within the PSC device (Fig. 3(g)), which can be fitted according to the equation: \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}=\) \(n k T\mathrm{ln}(I)/e\,+\) constant, where \(n\) is the ideal factor related to the dominant recombination mechanism in the device, \(k\) is the Boltzmann constant, \(T\) is the absolute temperature, and \(e\) refers to the elementary charge [38]. In particular, when an ideal factor \((n)\) is larger than 1, the additional trap-assisted Shockley–Read–Hall (SRH) recombination occurs, and decrease in the slope \((n k T/e)\) suggests suppression of the monomolecular SRH recombination [38]. Upon PCBDMAM modification, the \(n\) value decreases obviously from 1.34 (control) to 1.19 \((2~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) PCBDMAM). This confirms suppression of trap-assisted carrier recombination resulted from trap states passivation as discussed above.
The effect of PCBDMAM incorporation on the interfacial charge transport behavior of the device is probed by EIS. The Nyquist plots of device with and without PCBDMAM measured in dark under a reverse potential of \(1.0\,\mathrm{V}\) are compared in Fig. 3(h). According to a comparison of the corresponding fitted parameters based on an equivalent circuit model shown in the inset of Fig. 3h (see Table S6 in the ESM) [39], the \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\) (series resistance) value decreases slightly from 2.37 to \(1.95\;\;\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) after \(2~\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}m L^{-1}}\) PCBDMAM incorporation, whereas the \(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\) (charge transfer resistance) value decreases dramatically from 179.5 to \(78.0\ \ \Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) . These suggest that PCBDMAM incorporation facilitates interfacial charge transport, contributing directly to the increase of FF [39, 40].
