To disclose the reason responsible for the increase of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}},\) it is also necessary to check whether PCBDMAM incorporation improves the energy level alignment between \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) and \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite. We employed ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) to determine the valence band maximum (VBM), conduction band minimum (CBM) and work function \((W_{\mathrm{F}})\) of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) and \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite (see Figs. S12 and S13 and Table S7 in the ESM) [34, 41]. The LUMO energy level of PCBDMAM calculated from the onset reduction potential \((E_{\mathrm{red}}^{\mathrm{onset}})\) of PCBDMAM was \(-3.85\;\mathrm{eV}\) (see Fig. S14(a) in the ESM). On the basis of the optical band gap measured by UV–vis absorption onset of \((1.73\mathrm{~eV})\) , the HOMO energy level of PCBDMAM was calculated to be \(-5.58\;\;\mathrm{eV}\) (see Fig. S14(b) in the ESM). Since LUMO of PCBDMAM lies between the CBM levels of \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) and \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite, a cascade energy level arrangement forms and benefits electron extraction from \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite to \(Z\mathrm{nO}\) ETL, leading to increased \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) [34, 42, 43].
