We then monitored the thermal stability of PSC devices by aging them under continuous heating at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in a glove box with \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere for \(700\,\mathrm{h}\) (Fig. 4(c)). It is worth mentioning that we used thermally stable dopant-free polymer donor material (PDM), poly[(2,6-(4,8-bis(5-(2-ethylhexyl)thiophene-2-yl)-benzo \([1,2\cdot\mathrm{b}\cdot4,5\cdot\mathrm{b}^{\cdot}]\) ]dithiophene)-)-alt-(5,5-(1,3'-d-2-thienyl-5'7'-bis(2-ethylhexyl) benzo[1',2'-c:4',5'-c']dithiophene-4,8-dione))] (PBDB-T) as HTL to substitute less stable Spiro-OMeTAD HTL for thermal stability measurement, and the device structure is \(\mathrm{FTO/ZnO/PCBDMAM/CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}/\mathrm{PBDB-T/MoO}_{3}/\mathrm{Ag}\). The control device decays rapidly and retains only \(\sim62\%\) of its initial PCE after \(460\,\mathrm{h}\) heating, while the PCE of PCBDMAM device maintains \(\sim80\%\) after \(600\,\mathrm{h}\) heating. The PCE decay is mainly resulted from the decreases of \(V_{\infty}\) and FF (see Fig. S15 in the ESM for the changes of each parameters), which is attributed to the transformation of \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite from the \(\alpha\)-phase to the \(\delta\)-phase [40, 45]. Since PCBDMAM can effectively passivate defects in \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite layer and act as an isolating layer between \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) and perovskite layers, dramatic improvement of the thermal stability of PSC device is achieved.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, a bis-dimethylamino-functionalized fullerene derivative PCBDMAM is incorporated as an interlayer between \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) ETL and all-inorganic \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite layer, resulting in synchronous defect passivations of both layers and consequently dramatic enhancements of efficiency and thermal stability of PSC devices. After PCBDMAM modification, PCBDMAM interacts with \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) via formation of \(\mathrm{Zn-N}\) ionic bonds, leading to effective passivation of the surface defects of \(\mathrm{ZnO}\) especially oxygen vacancies. In addition, PCBDMAM incorporation affords effective passivation of \(\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{I}}\) and \(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{Pb}}\) antisite
defects within the atop \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) perovskite layer as well via coordination bonding between the \(_\mathrm{N}\) atoms of PCBDMAM with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{3+}\). As a result, a champion PCE of \(17.04\%\) is obtained for the regular-structure planar \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{2.25}\mathrm{Br}_{0.75}\) PSC device, surpassing that of the control device \((15.44\%)\). Moreover, after PCBDMAM incorporation, PSC device maintains \(\sim80\%\) of its initial PCE after \(600\,\mathrm{h}\) heating at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) hot plate in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere, whereas a rapid degradation of PCE to \(\sim62\%\) after \(460\,\mathrm{h}\) heating is observed for the control device under identical conditions. Hence, PCBDMAM incorporation benefits dramatic improvements of both efficiency and thermal stability of PSC device. Our synchronous defect passivation strategy paves the way for high-efficiency and thermal stable all-inorganic \(\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Br}_{x}\) perovskite solar cells.
